-## Category
-
-Assign categories to scenarios for grouping and filtering in the reports. Categories appear as filterable tags in the search bar using the `@tag` syntax.
-
-| Framework | How to set |
-|---|---|
-| **xUnit** | `[Trait("Category", "smoke")]` attribute on the test method |
-| **NUnit** | `[Category("smoke")]` attribute on the test method |
-| **MSTest** | `[TestCategory("smoke")]` attribute on the test method |
-| **BDDfy** | `.WithTags("category:smoke")` |
-| **LightBDD** | `[Label("smoke")]` attribute on the scenario method |
-| **ReqNRoll** | `@category:smoke` tag on the Gherkin scenario or feature |
-
-In the HTML reports, categories are searchable using `@smoke` in the search bar. See [[Search Syntax › Tag Filtering (@tag)|Search-Syntax#tag-filtering-tag]].
+## Tags
+
+A tag is a name on a feature or on a scenario, given through the framework's own tagging. Each level keeps its own list, and a scenario's tags in the report are its feature's tags, then its own, each once.
+
+| Framework | Scenario tags | Feature tags |
+|---|---|---|
+| **xUnit v3** | `[Trait("Category", "smoke")]` on the test method | `[Trait("Category", "smoke")]` on the test class |
+| **xUnit v2** | `[Trait("Category", "smoke")]` on the test method | `[Trait("Category", "smoke")]` on the test class or one of its base classes |
+| **NUnit 4** | `[Category("smoke")]` on the test; for a `[TestCase]`, its `Category = "smoke"` and the method's `[Category]` | `[Category]` or `[TestFixture(Category = "smoke")]` on the fixture, `[Category]` on a `[SetUpFixture]` above it, and `[assembly: Category("smoke")]` |
+| **MSTest** | `[TestCategory("smoke")]`, or any other `TestCategoryBaseAttribute`, on the test method | the same on the test class (a base class's included) and on the assembly |
+| **TUnit** | `[Category("smoke")]` on the test method | `[Category]` on the test class (a base class's included) and on the assembly |
+| **BDDfy** | the scenario's `.WithTags("smoke")`, then the xUnit test's `Category` traits | none: a story has no tags |
+| **LightBDD** | `[Label("smoke")]` and `[ScenarioCategory("smoke")]` on the scenario method | `[Label]` on the feature class |
+| **ReqNRoll** | the scenario's own Gherkin tags, its `Rule:`'s and its `Examples:` block's | the `Feature:`'s tags (Reqnroll's `FeatureInfo.Tags`) |
+| **Cucumber Messages** (`kronikol ingest`) | the scenario's own Gherkin tags, its `Rule:`'s and its `Examples:` block's | the `Feature:`'s tags |
+| **Tests NDJSON** (`kronikol ingest`) | the `tags` of the test's `start` record | the tags every scenario of the feature carries |
+
+- **The `category:` prefix is read and dropped.** `@category:smoke` in Gherkin and `.WithTags("category:smoke")` in BDDfy are the tag `smoke`, with no warning, so a scenario tagged both `@smoke` and `@category:smoke` has one tag. A `category:` value is a tag whatever it spells: `@category:happy-path` is the tag `happy-path`, not the happy path.
+- **Kronikol's markers are not tags.** A happy-path name sets the happy-path flag (see [Happy Path](#happy-path)) and `endpoint:/x` sets the feature's endpoint. No xUnit trait key other than `Category` is read, and no NUnit or TUnit property, so `[HappyPath]`, `[Endpoint]` and a trait such as `[Trait("Produces", "openapi.json")]` add no tag.
+- **Case.** Tags that differ only in the case of an ASCII letter are one tag (`Smoke` and `smoke`), spelled as the report first meets it. Other letters do not fold: `Über` and `über` are two tags.
+- **A feature's tags count for every one of its scenarios** and are drawn once, on the feature. A tag a scenario repeats from its feature is the feature's and is not drawn again on the scenario.
+- **Gherkin.** A `Rule:`'s tags reach its scenarios and no others. The happy path is read from a scenario's own tags and its `Examples:` block's only.
+- **LightBDD.** `[HappyPath]` sets the flag and is not a tag. A `[ScenarioCategory]` on the class reaches every scenario without saying which level it came from, so it stays on each scenario.
+- **xUnit v2** reads the traits on both of its capture paths, the test framework's results sink and the `[TestTracking]` attribute. Assembly-level traits are not read.
+- **Retries.** A retried scenario shows one retry badge, drawn from its attempt (`retry 2` for a third attempt). The ingest lanes no longer add `retry N` labels, so an author's own `retry 2` tag is an ordinary tag.
+
+### In the report
+
+- The filter box has one **Tags** row: a chip per tag with the number of scenarios carrying it, Happy Path first, and **Untagged** for the scenarios with no tag (a happy path counts as tagged). A click cycles a chip through has, has not and off; Alt+click or right-click sets has not. The row's ANY/ALL switch says whether a scenario needs any of the chosen tags or all of them. It starts on ANY, or where `ReportToggleDefaults.TagFilterMode` sets it ([[Report Configuration › Toggle Default Start States|Report-Configuration#toggle-default-start-states]]).
+- Choosing a feature's tag picks every scenario of that feature.
+- A row of more than 30 tags starts with the 30 the most scenarios carry and an "N more" chip that shows the rest.
+- `@smoke` in the search bar reads the same tags as the row, Happy Path included, ignoring case, and `@"two words"` finds a tag with a space. See [[Search Syntax › Tag Filtering (@tag)|Search-Syntax#tag-filtering-tag]].
+- Each scenario's element carries `data-tags`: its tags, each percent-encoded, joined with commas. It replaces `data-labels` and `data-categories`.
+- A link to a filtered report names the tags in `tags=` (has), `tagx=` (has not) and `tagmode=` ([[Generated Reports › Deep links|Generated-Reports#deep-links-scenario--and-sid-]]).
+
+Before 5.0.0 a scenario had two lists, labels and categories. Only a category had a button in the report (in a Categories row), only LightBDD and ReqNRoll made categories, and the xUnit, NUnit, MSTest and TUnit adapters read neither. [[Migrating to v5: tags|Migrating-to-v5-Tags]] lists what the change means for code, data files and links.
 
 To mark the tests a pull request added or edited, as worked out by your own detector and without changing the test code, see [[Marking New and Changed Tests]].
 
-## Labels
-
-Labels provide additional metadata attached to features and scenarios. In LightBDD, labels are set via `[Label("...")]` attributes and appear in the report alongside the scenario name. In ReqNRoll/BDDfy, tags that don't match the `happy-path`, `endpoint:`, or `category:` prefixes are treated as general labels.
-
 ## DocString
 
 Gherkin DocString content (triple-quoted blocks in `.feature` files) is captured as part of scenario steps and rendered as `<pre>` code blocks in the HTML reports. The optional media type (e.g. `json`, `xml`) specified after the opening `"""` delimiter controls syntax highlighting in the rendered output.
